Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from the book
Draft date: 7/30/2026
Notes: Period live-action Victorian London; cold blues and candle gold; one consistent human cast throughout

FADE IN:

EXT. LONDON - COUNTING-HOUSE DOOR - CHRISTMAS EVE

Fog and frost. Above a warehouse door, the painted legend: SCROOGE AND MARLEY. Snow sifts past the letters.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Marley was dead: to begin with. There is no doubt whatever about that. Old Marley was as dead as a door-nail.

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

A squeezing, wrenching, grasping, scraping, clutching, covetous old sinner at his desk: EBENEZER SCROOGE—thin blue lips, frosty rime on brow and chin, eyes red, pointed nose. A meagre fire. Beyond, in a dismal little cell, BOB CRATCHIT copies letters at a fire of one coal, white comforter about his neck.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Oh! But he was a tight-fisted hand at the grind-stone, Scrooge! Hard and sharp as flint. External heat and cold had little influence on Scrooge. No warmth could warm him.

The street door opens. FRED—ruddy, handsome, breath smoking—bursts in, all glow.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? What reason have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
Come, then. What right have you to be dismal? What reason have you to be morose? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Don't be cross, uncle!

SCROOGE
What else can I be, when I live in such a world of fools as this? If I could work my will, every idiot who goes about with "Merry Christmas" on his lips should be boiled with his own pudding, and buried with a stake of holly through his heart.

FRED
Uncle!

SCROOGE
Nephew! Keep Christmas in your own way, and let me keep it in mine.

FRED
Keep it! But you don't keep it.

FRED
I am sure I have always thought of Christmas time as a good time; a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time; the only time I know of, in the long calendar of the year, when men and women seem by one consent to open their shut-up hearts freely. And therefore, uncle, though it has never put a scrap of gold or silver in my pocket, I believe that it has done me good, and will do me good; and I say, God bless it!

Bob involuntarily applauds—then snuffs his last spark in terror.

SCROOGE
Let me hear another sound from you, and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation!

FRED
Don't be angry, uncle. Come! Dine with us to-morrow.

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED
I want nothing from you; I ask nothing of you; why cannot we be friends?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED
A Merry Christmas, uncle! And A Happy New Year!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

Fred leaves without anger; pauses to wish Bob the season. Bob returns it cordially.

SCROOGE
(muttering)
My clerk, with fifteen shillings a week, and a wife and family, talking about a merry Christmas. I'll retire to Bedlam.

Two PORTLY GENTLEMEN enter with books and papers, hats off.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Scrooge and Marley's, I believe. Have I the pleasure of addressing Mr. Scrooge, or Mr. Marley?

SCROOGE
Mr. Marley has been dead these seven years. He died seven years ago, this very night.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
At this festive season of the year, Mr. Scrooge, it is more than usually desirable that we should make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute, who suffer greatly at the present time. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Nothing!

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
You wish to be anonymous?

SCROOGE
I wish to be left alone. I don't make merry myself at Christmas and I can't afford to make idle people merry. Are there no prisons?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? Are they still in operation?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
They are. Still.

SCROOGE
The Treadmill and the Poor Law are in full vigour, then?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Both very busy, sir.

SCROOGE
Oh! I was afraid, from what you said at first, that something had occurred to stop them in their useful course. I'm very glad to hear it. If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population. It's not my business. Mine occupies me constantly. Good afternoon, gentlemen!

The gentlemen withdraw. Scrooge resumes work, improved in his own opinion.

Later: shutting time. Scrooge buttons his great-coat.

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB CRATCHIT
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient, and it's not fair. A poor excuse for picking a man's pocket every twenty-fifth of December! But I suppose you must have the whole day. Be here all the earlier next morning.

Bob promises. Scrooge growls out. Bob snuffs his candle, winds his long comforter, and runs for home.

EXT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

A gloomy pile up a dark yard. Fog and frost hang on the black gateway. Scrooge gropes to his door, key in hand—

—and the knocker is MARLEY'S FACE: livid, spectacles on ghostly forehead, hair stirred as by hot air, eyes wide and motionless.

Scrooge stares. It is a knocker again.

He turns the key sturdily, lights his candle, looks behind the door—nothing but screws—and bangs it shut.

SCROOGE
Pooh, pooh!

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

Sitting-room, bedroom, lumber-room. Small fire. Saucepan of gruel on the hob. Scrooge in dressing-gown, slippers, nightcap, double-locks himself in and sits to his gruel.

A disused bell begins to swing—softly, then loudly. Every bell in the house answers. Then silence.

A clanking from deep below—as of a heavy chain dragged over casks. Louder. Up the stairs. Straight toward the door.

SCROOGE
It's humbug still! I won't believe it.

Without a pause the thing comes through the heavy door. The dying flame leaps up.

JACOB MARLEY stands before him: pigtail, waistcoat, tights and boots; transparent; a chain clasped about his middle, made of cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers, deeds, and heavy purses wrought in steel. Kerchief bound about head and chin.

SCROOGE
How now! What do you want with me?

MARLEY
Much!

SCROOGE
Who are you?

MARLEY
Ask me who I was.

SCROOGE
Who were you then?

MARLEY
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
Can you—can you sit down?

MARLEY
I can.

Marley sits opposite the fire. Hair, skirts, and tassels still agitated as by hot vapour.

MARLEY
You don't believe in me.

SCROOGE
I don't.

MARLEY
Why do you doubt your senses?

SCROOGE
Because a little thing affects them. You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese, a fragment of an underdone potato. There's more of gravy than of grave about you, whatever you are!

Marley raises a frightful cry and shakes his chain. He takes off the bandage; his lower jaw drops upon his breast. Scrooge falls upon his knees.

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY
Man of the worldly mind! do you believe in me or not?

SCROOGE
I do. I must. But why do spirits walk the earth, and why do they come to me?

MARLEY
It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellowmen, and travel far and wide; and if that spirit goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death.

SCROOGE
You are fettered. Tell me why?

MARLEY
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard; I girded it on of my own free will, and of my own free will I wore it. Is its pattern strange to you?

Scrooge trembles.

MARLEY
Or would you know the weight and length of the strong coil you bear yourself? It was full as heavy and as long as this, seven Christmas Eves ago. You have laboured on it, since. It is a ponderous chain!

SCROOGE
Jacob. Old Jacob Marley, tell me more. Speak comfort to me, Jacob!

MARLEY
I have none to give. I cannot rest, I cannot stay, I cannot linger anywhere. In life my spirit never roved beyond the narrow limits of our money-changing hole; and weary journeys lie before me!

SCROOGE
But you were always a good man of business, Jacob.

MARLEY
Business! Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence, were, all, my business. The dealings of my trade were but a drop of water in the comprehensive ocean of my business!

He flings the chain upon the ground.

MARLEY
At this time of the rolling year, I suffer most. Hear me! My time is nearly gone. I am here to-night to warn you, that you have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate. A chance and hope of my procuring, Ebenezer.

SCROOGE
You were always a good friend to me. Thank'ee!

MARLEY
You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

SCROOGE
I—I think I'd rather not.

MARLEY
Without their visits, you cannot hope to shun the path I tread. Expect the first to-morrow, when the bell tolls One. Expect the second on the next night at the same hour. The third upon the next night when the last stroke of Twelve has ceased to vibrate. Look to see me no more; and look that, for your own sake, you remember what has passed between us!

Marley binds the wrapper round his head. He walks backward; the window rises of itself. He beckons. Confused noises of lamentation fill the air. Marley floats out upon the bleak dark night.

Scrooge at the window: the air filled with phantoms in chains, moaning, seeking to interfere for good and having lost the power forever. They fade into mist.

Scrooge closes the window, examines the double-locked door, tries to say "Humbug," and fails. He falls asleep upon the instant.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Darkness. Church chimes. The heavy bell strikes ONE—deep, dull, hollow, melancholy.

Light flashes. The bed-curtains are drawn aside by a hand.

THE GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST: like a child yet like an old man; white hair, unwrinkled face, tunic of purest white, lustrous belt, branch of holly, dress trimmed with summer flowers; from its head a bright clear jet of light; under its arm a great extinguisher for a cap. The figure fluctuates—now one arm, now twenty legs—then clear again.

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit, sir, whose coming was foretold to me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am!

SCROOGE
Who, and what are you?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long Past?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past.

SCROOGE
What business brings you here?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your welfare! Your reclamation, then. Take heed! Rise! and walk with me!

It clasps his arm. They move toward the window. Scrooge clings to his robe.

SCROOGE
I am a mortal, and liable to fall.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Bear but a touch of my hand there, and you shall be upheld in more than this!

The Spirit's hand upon his heart. They pass through the wall.

EXT. COUNTRY ROAD - DAY

Clear cold winter day; snow on the ground. Fields. A little market-town in the distance.

SCROOGE
Good Heaven! I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your lip is trembling. And what is that upon your cheek?

SCROOGE
(catching)
A pimple. Lead me where you will.

Boys on ponies and in carts shout Merry Christmas and part at cross-roads. Scrooge's cold eye glistens.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
These are but shadows of the things that have been. They have no consciousness of us. The school is not quite deserted. A solitary child, neglected by his friends, is left there still.

INT. BOARDING SCHOOL ROOM - DAY

Long bare melancholy room; deal forms and desks. A lonely BOY SCROOGE reads near a feeble fire. Grown Scrooge sits and weeps.

Figures of story spring at the window—Ali Baba, Valentine and Orson, a green parrot.

SCROOGE
Why, it's Ali Baba! Dear old honest Ali Baba! Poor boy!

(to himself, after drying his eyes)
I wish—but it's too late now. There was a boy singing a Christmas Carol at my door last night. I should like to have given him something: that's all.

The Ghost smiles and waves its hand.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Let us see another Christmas!

The boy grows larger. The room darker. A little girl—FAN—darts in, arms about his neck.

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! Home, for good and all. Home, for ever and ever. Father is so much kinder than he used to be. And you're to be a man! And first, we're to be together all the Christmas long, and have the merriest time in all the world.

BOY SCROOGE
You are quite a woman, little Fan!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Always a delicate creature. But she had a large heart!

SCROOGE
So she had. God forbid I should gainsay it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
She died a woman, and had, as I think, children.

SCROOGE
One child.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
True. Your nephew!

Scrooge looks uneasy.

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - NIGHT

An old gentleman in a Welsh wig behind a high desk: MR. FEZZIWIG. Clock points to seven.

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! Ebenezer! Dick! No more work to-night. Christmas Eve, Dick. Christmas, Ebenezer! Let's have the shutters up before a man can say Jack Robinson!

Young Scrooge and DICK WILKINS fly at the shutters. The warehouse becomes a snug bright ball-room. Fiddler on the desk. MRS. FEZZIWIG—one vast substantial smile—the three Miss Fezziwigs, apprentices, housemaid, cook, milkman—in they all come. Dancing, cake, cold roast, cold boiled, mince-pies. Fezziwig and Mrs. Fezziwig lead Sir Roger de Coverley; his calves shine like moons; he "cuts" so deftly he seems to wink with his legs.

At eleven, Mr. and Mrs. Fezziwig shake every hand and wish each a Merry Christmas.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter to make these silly folks so full of gratitude. He has spent but a few pounds of your mortal money: three or four perhaps.

SCROOGE
It isn't that, Spirit. He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome; a pleasure or a toil. The happiness he gives is quite as great as if it cost a fortune.

(feeling the Spirit's glance)
I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now. That's all.

EXT. OPEN AIR / INT. BELLE'S PARLOUR - NIGHT

Younger man Scrooge—prime of life, care and avarice beginning in the eye—beside a fair young girl in mourning: BELLE. Tears in her eyes.

BELLE
It matters little. To you, very little. Another idol has displaced me.

YOUNG MAN SCROOGE
What Idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one.

YOUNG MAN SCROOGE
This is the even-handed dealing of the world! There is nothing on which it is so hard as poverty; and there is nothing it professes to condemn with such severity as the pursuit of wealth!

BELLE
You fear the world too much. I have seen your nobler aspirations fall off one by one, until the master-passion, Gain, engrosses you. Our contract is an old one. It was made when we were both poor and content to be so. You are changed. When it was made, you were another man.

YOUNG MAN SCROOGE
I was a boy.

BELLE
Your own feeling tells you that you were not what you are. If this had never been between us, would you seek me out and try to win me now? Ah, no! I release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were. May you be happy in the life you have chosen!

She leaves him. They part.

SCROOGE
Spirit! show me no more! Conduct me home. Why do you delight to torture me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
One shadow more!

Another room: Belle, now a comely matron, opposite her daughter; a tumult of children; a husband home with Christmas toys. By the fire:

HUSBAND
Belle, I saw an old friend of yours this afternoon. Mr. Scrooge. His partner lies upon the point of death, I hear; and there he sat alone. Quite alone in the world, I do believe.

SCROOGE
Spirit! remove me from this place. I cannot bear it! Leave me! Take me back. Haunt me no longer!

He seizes the extinguisher-cap and presses it down upon the Spirit's head. Light streams from under it. Exhaustion. His own bedroom. Heavy sleep.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

The bell upon the stroke of One. A blaze of ruddy light from the adjoining room. Scrooge shuffles to the door. A strange voice calls his name.

He enters. His room transformed: living green, holly, mistletoe, ivy; a mighty blaze; heaped on the floor a throne of turkeys, geese, game, sausages, mince-pies, plum-puddings, oysters, apples, oranges, seething bowls of punch.

Upon this couch: the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT—a jolly Giant in a green robe bordered with white fur, bare breast, bare feet, holly wreath with icicles, glowing torch like Plenty's horn, antique empty scabbard eaten with rust.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! and know me better, man! I am the Ghost of Christmas Present. Look upon me! You have never seen the like of me before!

SCROOGE
Never.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Have never walked forth with the younger members of my family—meaning my elder brothers born in these later years?

SCROOGE
I am afraid I have not. Have you had many brothers, Spirit?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
More than eighteen hundred.

SCROOGE
A tremendous family to provide for! Spirit, conduct me where you will. I went forth last night on compulsion, and I learnt a lesson which is working now. To-night, if you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

Scrooge holds fast. Room, fire, feast vanish.

EXT. LONDON STREETS - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Snow on roofs; people scraping pavements; cheerful despite gloom. Poulterers' and fruiterers' radiant. Church bells. Poor people carry dinners to the bakers'. The Spirit sprinkles incense from his torch upon their dinners; angry words dissolve into good humour.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
To any kindly given. To a poor one most. Because it needs it most.

They pass on, invisible, to the suburbs.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - DAY

Four-roomed house. MRS. CRATCHIT—poor gown, brave in ribbons—lays the cloth with BELINDA. MASTER PETER plunges a fork into potatoes, monstrous shirt-collar choking him. Two smaller Cratchits scream of the goose. MARTHA arrives from work; hides as a joke.

BOB CRATCHIT enters—threadbare clothes brushed, at least three feet of comforter—TINY TIM upon his shoulder. Tiny Tim bears a little crutch; iron frame supports his limbs.

BOB CRATCHIT
Why, where's our Martha?

MRS. CRATCHIT
Not coming.

BOB CRATCHIT
Not coming upon Christmas Day!

Martha runs into his arms. The young Cratchits bear Tiny Tim off to hear the pudding sing.

MRS. CRATCHIT
And how did little Tim behave?

BOB CRATCHIT
As good as gold, and better. He told me, coming home, that he hoped the people saw him in the church, because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to them to remember upon Christmas Day who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

Bob's voice trembles. Tiny Tim's crutch on the floor; back to his stool before the fire.

Goose procession. Grace. Mrs. Cratchit plunges the knife; stuffing issues; a murmur of delight. Tiny Tim beats with his knife-handle.

TINY TIM
Hurrah!

Pudding like a speckled cannon-ball, blazing in ignited brandy, holly on top. Apples, oranges, chestnuts. Hot mixture in a jug. Bob serves with beaming looks.

BOB CRATCHIT
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

ALL
God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

He sits close to Bob; Bob holds his withered little hand.

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner, carefully preserved. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no. Oh, no, kind Spirit! say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, none other of my race will find him here. What then? If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge hangs his head at his own words.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Man, if man you be in heart, not adamant, forbear that wicked cant until you have discovered What the surplus is, and Where it is. Will you decide what men shall live, what men shall die? It may be that in the sight of Heaven you are more worthless and less fit to live than millions like this poor man's child.

BOB CRATCHIT
Mr. Scrooge! I'll give you Mr. Scrooge, the Founder of the Feast!

MRS. CRATCHIT
The Founder of the Feast indeed! I wish I had him here. I'd give him a piece of my mind to feast upon. It should be Christmas Day on which one drinks the health of such an odious, stingy, hard, unfeeling man as Mr. Scrooge.

BOB CRATCHIT
My dear, Christmas Day.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I'll drink his health for your sake and the Day's, not for his. Long life to him! A merry Christmas and a happy new year! He'll be very merry and very happy, I have no doubt!

The shadow of Scrooge's name passes. They grow merrier. Tiny Tim sings a plaintive little song. The Spirit's torch brightens them as they fade. Scrooge's eye stays on Tiny Tim until the last.

EXT. OPEN PLACE - NIGHT

The Ghost has grown older; hair grey. Chimes ring three quarters past eleven.

SCROOGE
Are spirits' lives so short?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
My life upon this globe is very brief. It ends to-night. To-night at midnight. Hark! The time is drawing near.

SCROOGE
Forgive me if I am not justified in what I ask, but I see something strange protruding from your skirts. Is it a foot or a claw?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
It might be a claw, for the flesh there is upon it. Look here.

From the robe: two children, wretched, abject, frightful—yellow, meagre, ragged, scowling, wolfish. A boy and a girl, clinging.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Oh, Man! look here. Look, look, down here! This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, and all of their degree, but most of all beware this boy, for on his brow I see that written which is Doom, unless the writing be erased.

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The bell strikes twelve. The Ghost is gone.

A solemn Phantom, draped and hooded, comes like a mist along the ground: the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME. Deep black garment; nothing visible save one outstretched hand.

SCROOGE
I am in the presence of the Ghost of Christmas Yet To Come? You are about to show me shadows of the things that have not happened, but will happen in the time before us. Is that so, Spirit?

The garment contracts as if the Spirit nods.

SCROOGE
Ghost of the Future! I fear you more than any spectre I have seen. But as I know your purpose is to do me good, and as I hope to live to be another man from what I was, I am prepared to bear you company, and do it with a thankful heart. Lead on! Lead on! The night is waning fast, and it is precious time to me. Lead on, Spirit!

EXT. THE EXCHANGE - DAY

Merchants hurry, chink money, converse. The Spirit points. Scrooge listens.

FAT MERCHANT
No, I don't know much about it, either way. I only know he's dead.

MERCHANT HALES
When did he die?

FAT MERCHANT
Last night, I believe.

MERCHANT KEMP
What was the matter with him? I thought he'd never die.

FAT MERCHANT
God knows. It's likely to be a very cheap funeral; for upon my life I don't know of anybody to go to it. Suppose we make up a party and volunteer?

RED-FACED GENTLEMAN
I don't mind going if a lunch is provided. But I must be fed, if I make one.

They laugh and stroll away. Elsewhere, two wealthy men:

MR. CHAPMAN
Old Scratch has got his own at last, hey?

MR. PELL
So I am told. Cold, isn't it? Seasonable for Christmas time. Good morning!

Not another word.

INT. OLD JOE'S RAG-AND-BOTTLE SHOP - NIGHT

Low-browed shop; iron, rags, bottles, bones. OLD JOE by a charcoal stove. A CHARWOMAN with a heavy bundle; MRS. DILBER the laundress; an UNDERTAKER'S MAN in faded black. They burst into a laugh.

CHARWOMAN
Let the charwoman alone to be the first! Look here, old Joe, here's a chance! If we haven't all three met here without meaning it!

OLD JOE
You couldn't have met in a better place. Come into the parlour.

Bundles opened: seal, pencil-case, sleeve-buttons; sheets, towels, teaspoons; then—

OLD JOE
What do you call this? Bed-curtains!

CHARWOMAN
Ah! Bed-curtains! Why not? I certainly shan't hold my hand, when I can get anything in it by reaching it out, for the sake of such a man as He was. His blankets? He isn't likely to take cold without 'em. Somebody was fool enough to put his best shirt on him to be buried in, but I took it off again. Calico's quite as becoming to the body. He can't look uglier than he did in that one.

OLD JOE
(counting money from a flannel bag)
That's your account.

CHARWOMAN
Ha, ha! This is the end of it, you see! He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead! Ha, ha, ha!

SCROOGE
Spirit! I see, I see. The case of this unhappy man might be my own. My life tends that way, now. Merciful Heaven, what is this!

INT. DARK BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

A bare uncurtained bed. Beneath a ragged sheet, a body—plundered, bereft, unwatched, unwept, uncared for. Pale light on the bed. A cat tears at the door; rats gnaw beneath the hearth-stone.

The Spirit points to the head. Scrooge cannot lift the cover.

SCROOGE
Spirit! this is a fearful place. In leaving it, I shall not leave its lesson, trust me. Let us go! If there is any person in the town who feels emotion caused by this man's death, show that person to me, Spirit, I beseech you!

INT. DEBTORS' HOME - DAY

A mother, CAROLINE, anxious; children. Her husband enters—careworn, yet a serious delight he struggles to repress.

CAROLINE
Is it good, or bad?

HUSBAND
Bad.

CAROLINE
We are quite ruined?

HUSBAND
No. There is hope yet, Caroline. He is past relenting. He is dead.

She is thankful in her soul—then prays forgiveness. Their hearts are lighter. A happier house for this man's death.

SCROOGE
Let me see some tenderness connected with a death, or that dark chamber will be for ever present to me.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - DAY

Quiet. Very quiet. Mrs. Cratchit and the girls sew. Peter has a book. The noisy little Cratchits still as statues.

PETER
(reading)
"And He took a child, and set him in the midst of them."

MRS. CRATCHIT
The colour hurts my eyes. They're better now again. It must be near his time.

PETER
Past it rather. But I think he has walked a little slower than he used, these few last evenings, mother.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I have known him walk with Tiny Tim upon his shoulder, very fast indeed. But he was very light to carry, and his father loved him so, that it was no trouble: no trouble. And there is your father at the door!

Bob enters in his comforter. They help him to tea. He is cheerful—then breaks.

BOB CRATCHIT
My little, little child! My little child!

He goes upstairs to a room hung with Christmas; a chair beside the child. He kisses the little face, composes himself, comes down.

BOB CRATCHIT
I am sure we shall none of us forget poor Tiny Tim—shall we—or this first parting that there was among us?

ALL
Never, father!

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very happy. I am very happy!

They kiss him. Spirit of Tiny Tim—childish essence from God.

SCROOGE
Spectre, something informs me that our parting moment is at hand. Tell me what man that was whom we saw lying dead!

EXT. CHURCHYARD - DAY

Walled in by houses; grass and weeds; choked with too much burying. The Spirit stands among the graves and points down to one.

SCROOGE
Before I draw nearer to that stone to which you point, answer me one question. Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be, only? Men's courses will foreshadow certain ends, to which, if persevered in, they must lead. But if the courses be departed from, the ends will change. Say it is thus with what you show me!

The Spirit is immovable. Scrooge creeps toward the grave, trembling, and reads:

EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed? No, Spirit! Oh no, no!

The finger points from the grave to him and back again.

SCROOGE
Spirit! hear me! I am not the man I was. I will not be the man I must have been but for this intercourse. Why show me this, if I am past all hope!

The hand appears to shake.

SCROOGE
Good Spirit, your nature intercedes for me, and pities me. Assure me that I yet may change these shadows you have shown me, by an altered life! I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. I will not shut out the lessons that they teach. Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

He clutches the spectral hand. The Spirit repulses him. Holding up his hands in a last prayer, he sees the Phantom shrink, collapse, and dwindle down into a bedpost.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - MORNING

The bedpost is his own. The bed his own. The room his own. Best and happiest of all—the Time before him his own, to make amends in!

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future! The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh Jacob Marley! Heaven, and the Christmas Time be praised for this! I say it on my knees, old Jacob; on my knees!

He scrambles out, laughing and crying, wrestling with his stockings.

SCROOGE
I don't know what to do! I am as light as a feather, I am as happy as an angel, I am as merry as a schoolboy. I am as giddy as a drunken man. A merry Christmas to everybody! A happy New Year to all the world. Hallo here! Whoop! Hallo!

He frisks into the sitting-room—gruel saucepan, Marley's door, Present's corner, the window of wandering spirits.

SCROOGE
It's all right, it's all true, it all happened. Ha ha ha!

Church bells—clash, clang, hammer; ding, dong, bell—glorious. He runs to the window: no fog, no mist; clear, bright, jovial, cold; golden sunlight; sweet fresh air.

SCROOGE
What's to-day!

A BOY in Sunday clothes below.

BOY
Eh?

SCROOGE
What's to-day, my fine fellow?

BOY
To-day! Why, CHRISTMAS DAY.

SCROOGE
It's Christmas Day! I haven't missed it. The Spirits have done it all in one night. They can do anything they like. Hallo, my fine fellow! Do you know the Poulterer's, in the next street but one, at the corner? Do you know whether they've sold the prize Turkey that was hanging up there?—Not the little prize Turkey: the big one?

BOY
What, the one as big as me?

SCROOGE
What a delightful boy! It's a pleasure to talk to him. Yes, my buck!

BOY
It's hanging there now.

SCROOGE
Is it? Go and buy it, and tell 'em to bring it here, that I may give them the direction where to take it. Come back with the man, and I'll give you a shilling. Come back with him in less than five minutes and I'll give you half-a-crown!

The boy is off like a shot.

SCROOGE
(whispering, rubbing his hands)
I'll send it to Bob Cratchit's! He sha'n't know who sends it. It's twice the size of Tiny Tim!

He pats the knocker.

SCROOGE
I shall love it, as long as I live! What an honest expression it has in its face! It's a wonderful knocker!—Here's the Turkey! Hallo! Whoop! How are you! Merry Christmas!

EXT. LONDON STREETS - DAY

Scrooge dressed in his best, smiling at everyone. Good-humoured fellows return:

PASSERSBY
Good morning, sir! A merry Christmas to you!

He meets the Portly Gentleman of yesterday.

SCROOGE
My dear sir, how do you do? I hope you succeeded yesterday. It was very kind of you. A merry Christmas to you, sir! Allow me to ask your pardon. And will you have the goodness—

He whispers in the gentleman's ear.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Lord bless me! My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
If you please. Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it, I assure you. Will you come and see me?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
I will!

SCROOGE
Thank'ee. I am much obliged to you. Bless you!

INT. FRED'S DINING-ROOM - DAY

Scrooge sidles his face round the door. Table in great array. Fred and his pretty niece.

SCROOGE
Fred!

FRED
Why bless my soul! Who's that?

SCROOGE
It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Let him in! Hearty shaking of hands. Wonderful party, wonderful unanimity, wonderful happiness.

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

Next morning. Scrooge early, door wide open to the Tank. Clock strikes nine. A quarter past. Bob—full eighteen minutes and a half behind—hat off, comforter off, on his stool in a jiffy.

SCROOGE
(feigning the old growl)
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time.

SCROOGE
You are? Yes. I think you are. Step this way, sir, if you please.

BOB CRATCHIT
It's only once a year, sir. It shall not be repeated. I was making rather merry yesterday, sir.

SCROOGE
Now, I'll tell you what, my friend, I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer. And therefore—

He leaps from his stool and digs Bob in the waistcoat so that Bob staggers back into the Tank.

SCROOGE
and therefore I am about to raise your salary! A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas, Bob, my good fellow, than I have given you for many a year! I'll raise your salary, and endeavour to assist your struggling family, and we will discuss your affairs this very afternoon, over a Christmas bowl of smoking bishop, Bob! Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

EXT. LONDON - DAY

Snow bright. Scrooge with Tiny Tim upon his shoulder—Tim who did NOT die—walking like a second father. Bob beside them. Fred waves across the street. Church bells.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Scrooge was better than his word. He did it all, and infinitely more; and to Tiny Tim, who did NOT die, he was a second father. He became as good a friend, as good a master, and as good a man, as the good old city knew. Some people laughed to see the alteration in him, but he let them laugh. His own heart laughed: and that was quite enough for him. He had no further intercourse with Spirits, but lived upon the Total Abstinence Principle, ever afterwards; and it was always said of him, that he knew how to keep Christmas well, if any man alive possessed the knowledge. May that be truly said of us, and all of us!

Tiny Tim looks out at us, bright.

TINY TIM
God bless us, every one!

FADE OUT.

THE END
